{
  "schema": "splatthis.fidelity/7",
  "what": "Two different questions, kept apart because they have very different answers.\n\n`deployed`  -- the emitted artifact against the ORIGINAL image. This is what a user of this tool gets, and it is dominated by fitting error.\n`compositor` -- the emitted artifact against the internal reference render of the SAME splats. This isolates the emitter, and is only the relevant number for a caller that supplies its own splats (projecting 3D Gaussians through EWA, say) and therefore has no fitting error.",
  "why": "A consumer supplying its own splats -- projecting 3D Gaussians through EWA rather than fitting a bitmap -- inherits only this term, not the fitting error that dominates source-fidelity numbers.",
  "metrics": "**Read LPIPS. SSIM is published only for continuity and should not be used to judge these artifacts.** On this content SSIM has almost no dynamic range: measured against the reference render of one chameleon population, a 2-pixel Gaussian blur scores 0.9290 and the *source photograph* -- a different image entirely -- scores 0.9053, against 0.9336 for the actual SVG. A flat grey field still scores 0.6373. LPIPS separates the same four cases as 0.1268 / 0.1456 / 0.0411 / 0.9422. This is the blur bias CLAUDE.md warns about, and it is why the project gates on LPIPS.",
